-- Pass 100 §4 — `.` is DATA ACCESS only when it is GLUED to what it walks
-- from. `a.b` is the access; a leading ` .b` is the anchor walk and starts a
-- fresh expression. Adjacency was already a grammar fact here — `>>=` is `>>`
-- with an `=` glued to it, which `glued.duo` asserts — and this is the same
-- test applied to the other sigil §4 gives two readings.
--
-- WHY IT MATTERED: §20 writes `if .pos < #.src .src[.pos] else nil`. With no
-- adjacency test, BOTH dot loops — the suffix loop and
-- `parse_field_projection`'s own chain loop — read `#.src .src[.pos]` as
-- `#((.src).src[.pos])`, so the condition swallowed the then-expression and
-- the `else` had nothing in front of it.
--
-- WHAT THIS ASSERTS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. The three numbers cover both loops
-- I changed, and each one is a chain that a "stop consuming dots" fix would
-- have broken:
--
--   12  a two-level chain in ARGUMENT position — `parse_field_projection`'s
--       chain loop. A fix that stopped after one level answers nil.
--   3   ordinary one-level access — the suffix loop's common case.
--   12  the same two-level chain in ordinary expression position, so the two
--       loops are both shown reaching the same depth on the same data.
--
-- The SPLIT half — that ` .b` after a complete expression begins a new one —
-- is asserted by the `shc/lex.duo` row of the blocks table and NOT by a value
-- here, deliberately. A leading `.` still lowers to a closure rather than to
-- the field it names (rows 1b/1c of docs/anchor_stance_conflict.md, owned
-- elsewhere), so every value-level spelling of the split fails in the C
-- backend today for a reason that has nothing to do with adjacency. Asserting
-- it against a closure would be asserting the defect.
-- expect: 12 3 12

use = (f, v) f(v)

main = (): i64
    deep = { a = { b = 12 } }
    rec = { a = 3, b = 4 }
    arg = use(.a.b, deep)
    flat = rec.a
    walk = deep.a.b
    print("{arg} {flat} {walk}")
    0
end
